STRIDE & BASE | Issue 232 | May 8, 2026

THIS WEEK: A half-marathon block for people with real jobs

Most half-marathon plans are written for someone who can run at 6 AM every da=
y and nap at noon. This one is written for the rest of us: 6-7 sessions a wee=
k, one of them optional, and nothing that falls apart if you miss a day.

The structure:
- One long run, building from 90 minutes to 2 hours, with the last 20 minutes=
 at goal pace in weeks 6, 8 and 10.
- One threshold session: 3x10 minutes at about one-hour race effort, building=
 to 2x20.
- Everything else easy. Truly easy.
- A down week every fourth week, cutting volume by a third.

Why threshold and not intervals? For the half, the limiting factor is how fas=
t you can run before lactate starts piling up, and that number moves most wit=
h 20-40 minutes of comfortably hard running per week. VO2max intervals are a =
nice garnish in the last month.

For readers with a big aerobic base (lots of you wrote in after our base-phas=
e issue): you'll be surprised how quickly threshold pace comes around once yo=
u add it. Six weeks of one session a week is often worth 10-15 seconds per km.

GEAR
Race-day shoes with carbon plates: worth roughly 1-3% for most runners, more =
if your form holds up late. Train in them a few times first.

Download the full plan (PDF) at strideandbase.com/half12.

The Stride & Base editors

